just eight years ago exxon was the most  valuable company in the world boasting a  peak market cap of 446 billion dollars  but since then exxon has slowly slipped  down the list first they lost the number  one spot then they dropped out of the  top ten and now they've tumbled all the  way down to number 33 with a market cap  of 259 billion dollars that means that  exxon is officially over 10 times behind  the new market leader apple  to make things worse exxon has struggled  to regain profitability over the past  year and to top it all off exxon was  kicked out of the dow jones last year  the dow jones is a combination of the  top 30 american companies that meet a  set of guidelines and exxon had been  part of this list for 92 years  yet it looks like hitting a hundred  years in dao simply was not in the cards  for exon at first glance it's easy to  attribute this fall to the rise of evs  but evs only account for three percent  of global car sales so even though an eb  takeover is inevitable eevees aren't  really the ones eating intoxion's top  and bottom lines today  in fact exxon would likely be in a very  similar situation even if it wasn't  freebies so here's what happened to the  oil king that dominated the business  world for nearly a century  taking a look back the most obvious  factor behind exxon's fall is of course  crashing oil prices when your entire  business is based on oil you're  basically screwed if oil takes nose dive  and that's exactly what happened between  2014 and 2016.  within those two years oil prices  plummeted from 120 per barrel down to  just 40 per barrel this flash crash was  caught by a handful of factors most of  which exxon had no control over first of  all the us dollar was popping off  against the euro and given that  commodities are generally quoted in usd  a strengthening dollar meant flying  commodity prices  in the meantime china was actively  trying to do the exact opposite by  devaluing the yuan in 2015  you see it china likes to maintain a  weak currency on purpose and while that  might sound stupid there is reason  behind the madness when you have a weak  currency it's easier for foreigners to  forge your exports which increases  exports at the same time it also becomes  harder for citizens to afford imported  items which reduces overall imports  while this was a good thing for china  this was terrible for exxon given that  china is the world's largest oil  importer  all of a sudden exon had substantially  less revenue from americans and the  chinese and to make things worse the  middle eastern oil giants would screw  over exxon as well  given the reduced demand for oil it  would have been a smart move for opec to  decrease oil production  now a lot of you would argue that  decreasing production is an unethical  way to prop up prices but from their  side it would have been smart but they  actively decided not to reduce oil  production which led to a surplus and  oil which further drove down prices and  the nail in the coffin was the iran  nuclear deal which allowed iran to start  exporting oil once again  it's not clear how much of an impact  this additional supply actually had but  the threat itself was enough for  investors to flee oil all of these  factors combined led to exxon's revenue  crashing over 50 percent from 440  billion dollars down to 205 billion  dollars but falling oil prices was just  a taste of what was to come  usually with any investment the worst  case scenario is just losing everything  you put in  unless you're shorting a stock the worst  case scenario is just zero but with oil  prices things went beyond zero when all  prices literally went negative  no one really expected how quickly the  pandemic could shut down the entire  world and decrease all demand to nearly  zero so it's not surprising that  producers and refineries were left with  large amounts of excess oil once the  pandemic hit the problem though is that  you can't just throw the oil into some  reserves and try to sell it once demand  recovers  oil giants already had hundreds of  tankers carrying millions of barrels  worth of oil to their destinations but  once they reached their destination  customers no longer wanted the oil  so they literally had millions of  barrels of oil just floating around in  the ocean doing nothing and every day  these tankers were just sitting around  loads of money was being burned it was  just a matter of time until someone  broke and paid someone to simply take  away the oil and that's exactly what  happened on april 20th 2020. in just one  day crude oil futures fell 306 percent  from 18.27 cents to negative 37.63 cents  and just when you think that things  can't get any worse saudi arabia and  russia decide to have a price war common  sense would tell you that when oil  prices are plummeting and even going  negative it's a good time to heavily  reduce or even halt oil production  yet saudi arabia and russia decide to do  the exact opposite both of them knew  that the other was losing money and so  they tried to basically run each other  out of business  they did come to a truce eventually but  not before pumping up opec oil output to  30-year highs when prices were literally  negative  anyway exxon wasn't the leading voice  behind a 2014 oil crash the pandemic or  negative oil prices but they got  obliterated in the crossfire nothing  we've discussed so far is specific to  exxon and all oil companies were hurt by  these events but unlike exxon and shell  oil companies like chevron have almost  fully recovered  in fact chevron is only 10 to 15 away  from all-time highs and they never got  kicked from the dow jones so clearly a  lot of blame has to be placed on exxon  as well  probably the easiest loss exxon could  have avoided was making outsized bets on  the oil industry  even though eb's haven't made a big dent  in the overall oil industry yet it's  just a matter of time and investing  heavily into oil ventures is plain  stupid but that's exactly what exxon has  been doing over the past decade in 2009  they spent 41 billion dollars buying  extra energy in 2012 they spent 2.6  billion dollars buying celtic  exploration in 2017 they spent another  2.5 billion dollars requiring intra oil  and that same year they spent another  6.6 billion dollars to buy the best  family's permian assets  aside from making acquisitions left and  right exxon has also been investing  heavily into their own business  as recent as january 2020 the ceo of  exxon darren woods announced that exxon  will be investing 30 billion into  expiration projects in 2020 alone and  the plan was to spend 30 to 35 billion  dollars every year through 2025.  unfortunately for example all these poor  investments are only starting to bite  back after a decade for the longest time  exxon didn't want to admit that these  were bad investments they insisted that  these investments just needed time to  recover and they refused to value these  assets below what they paid for them but  recently exxon has been getting sued for  overvaluing their assets and the  misleading investors  exxon finally gave in at the end of 2020  and rolled off the 41 billion xto  investment as a 20 billion loss and this  right here is what really killed exxon  in 2020. even during the height of the  pandemic exxon was only losing half a  billion to one billion dollars per  quarter but after writing off this loss  in the last quarter of 2020 exxon posted  a 20 billion loss  and that marked the end of exxon's first  unprofitable year in 40 years  exxon isn't undeclared yet either there  are still multiple ongoing lawsuits  accusing exxon of overvaluing their  other assets  so last year's pain for write down may  just be the first of many  moving on to exxon's other big money  train we have their dividend payments  one of the defining characteristics of  exxon stock is their fallen history of  dividend payments  exxon has literally never missed a  different payment in their history  standard oil began paying a dividend in  1882 and exxon has kept up a tradition  ever since the breakup this means that  exxon didn't miss a payment through  world war one the great depression of  world war ii the inflation crisis 1973  oil crisis the dot-com bubble the  financial recession and of course the  pandemic this places exxon in an elite  group of stocks called dividend  aristocrats who not only never miss a  payment but continuously increase their  payments  it's not always super easy to pay a  dividend though especially a high  yielding dividend over the past few  years exxon has been forced to borrow  money to pay out their dividends and  while that's super irresponsible at this  point a lot of exxon's leadership would  rather file for bankruptcy than miss a  dividend payment and i don't blame them  if they miss a dividend payment not only  will they ruin a 140-year streak that's  virtually impossible to rebuild but they  would give up one of the only things  that's still going for exxon exxon may  be going bankrupt over the next decade  but one thing many investors are still  extremely confident in is that exxon  will pay dividends till the very end and  if exxon were to miss a payment right  now that would simply destroy investor  trust and drive down the stock price  even lower at least in the short term  with that being said though paying out a  massive dividend given the company's  situation is extremely irresponsible and  it's objectively holding back the  company  looking into the future it's not clear  where exxon is headed in terms of  financials exxon is nearly back to  pre-pandemic profit levels on a  quarterly basis but the thing to keep in  mind is that the pandemic wasn't what  hurt exxon the most it was actually  writing down that 20 billion investment  loss and given that exxon has plenty of  other such investments it's very  possible that they continue to increase  its losses over the next decade in the  meantime exxon refuses to embrace  renewable energy exxon is actually  currently being investigated for  spreading disinformation regarding  climate change and downplaying its  effects when questioned in court does  the euro darren woods of course deny  these allegations but whether exxon is  spreading this information or not their  stance on the future is crystal clear  darren woods has repeatedly reiterated  that climate change does not pose any  long-term threats to the oil industry in  my opinion this viewpoint is  fundamentally flawed as the transition  to renewable energy has little to do  with climate change now don't get me  wrong here switching to renewable energy  will help with combating climate change  but climate change is not what's pushing  the transition itself the primary  factors that will move the needle in my  opinion is electric options being more  convenient easier to maintain and most  importantly cheaper once these demands  are met companies and people will switch  to renewable options regardless of  whether they believe in climate change  or not now it's not like this change is  going to happen overnight and axon is  going to go bankrupt next year but the  longer exxon chooses to ignore this  trend the more likely it is that they'll  end up like texaco but that's just what  i think do you guys think exxon has a  future comment that down below  also drop a like if you're glad that the  era of all giants is coming to an end  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest a few video  ideas and consider subscribing and see  more questions logically answered but  until then i'm ari and i'll see you guys  on the next one  you
 

